--TEST--
every shape of array-element write through a hooked property is refused where it is written, PHP's "indirect modification of overloaded property" taken as a compile error — `rule:classes/property-hooks`, `rule:types/arrays`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:classes/property-hooks` makes a hooked property a pair of accessors and not a slot, so
// the copy `rule:types/arrays` separates on a write has nowhere to be written back
// to. PHP agrees at run time — `Error: Indirect modification of Grid::$rows is
// not allowed` — so refusing at compile time is the compatible answer, not a
// divergence. All four subscript spellings take the same refusal, because the
// lowering flattens every one of them down to the same root holder.
class Grid {
    public array<string> $rows {
        get => $this->rows;
        set (array<string> $v) {
            $this->rows = $v;
        }
    }

    public array<array<string>> $cells {
        get => $this->cells;
        set (array<array<string>> $v) {
            $this->cells = $v;
        }
    }

    public function constructor() {
        array<string> $flat = [];
        $this->rows = $flat;
        array<array<string>> $seed = [];
        $this->cells = $seed;
    }
}

var $g = new Grid();
$g->rows["0"] = "z";
$g->cells["0"]["1"] = "z";
$g->rows["0"] .= "x";
$g->rows[] = "appended";
echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0478]: an array element cannot be written through the hooked property `rows`
   --> case.nvs:32:1
%A
error[E0478]: an array element cannot be written through the hooked property `cells`
   --> case.nvs:33:1
%A
error[E0478]: an array element cannot be written through the hooked property `rows`
   --> case.nvs:34:1
%A
error[E0478]: an array element cannot be written through the hooked property `rows`
   --> case.nvs:35:1
